# Target C-II — exact-phase marker intervals are two-sided diameter anchored through `t<=1.8687`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only structural corollary  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Depends on:** phase-zero marker interval isolation through `1.8687`; Type-II diameter-normal convergence  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The marker-path theorem classifies every rigid proper interval block whose two coherent boundary train directions are exactly equal.  This note records a geometric consequence that is useful at source level: the two bars in the forced TB2 certificate anchor the moving block on opposite loaded-hull marker arcs.

Let `theta_+,theta_-` be the fixed antipodal phase markers and let `tau_i,tau_j`, `i<j`, be two surviving marker-path cuts with the same coherent real direction

\[
\nu_i=\nu_j=\nu.
\]

Let `R_ij` be the path block between them. Then there exist two distinct actual diameter edges `h_+,h_-`, each with exactly one endpoint in `R_ij`, such that after orienting each diameter vector from its fixed endpoint into the moving block,

\[
(n_{h_+}\cdot J\nu)(n_{h_-}\cdot J\nu)<0.
\]

Consequently the two moving diameter endpoints lie on opposite open marker-to-marker loaded-hull arcs.  In particular an exact-phase block cannot have all of its diameter anchoring on only one side of the fixed marker pair.

For an exact-phase run of consecutive path cuts

\[
\tau_a,\tau_{a+1},\ldots,\tau_b,
\qquad \nu_a=\cdots=\nu_b=\nu,
\]

with all intervening regions nonhub, every region `R_k` between `tau_k,tau_{k+1}` contains at least one actual-diameter endpoint on each of the two marker-to-marker loaded-hull arcs. Since distinct path regions have disjoint interiors and disjoint assigned loaded-hull subarcs, a run of `r=b-a` exact-phase interfaces forces at least `r` distinct actual-diameter endpoints on each marker arc.

## Proof

For one proper interval block, the hostile-audited marker isolation theorem gives the exact equal-phase alternative: TB1 is impossible and every inclusion-minimal tangent obstruction is a TB2 pair `h_+,h_-` with opposite strict transverse signs

\[
(n_{h_+}\cdot J\nu)(n_{h_-}\cdot J\nu)<0.
\]

Minimality makes the two diameter edges distinct. By construction each is block-crossing, hence has exactly one moving endpoint in `R_ij`.

The marker deletion theorem also places every moving diameter endpoint outside shrinking neighborhoods of `theta_+,theta_-`, and uniform Type-II diameter-normal convergence identifies `n_h/D` with the outward loaded-hull normal at that moving endpoint up to `o(rho_D)`. On the oriented normal circle, deleting the antipodal marker pair leaves exactly two open semicircular arcs. The sign of `n\cdot J\nu` is constant on each arc and opposite on the two arcs. Therefore opposite strict transverse signs force the two moving endpoints onto opposite marker-to-marker arcs.

Now take an exact-phase consecutive run. Apply the preceding argument to each adjacent block `R_k`. The loaded-hull boundary assigned to different path regions consists of disjoint consecutive subarcs on each marker-to-marker side. Hence the selected endpoint on a given side for `R_k` cannot equal the selected endpoint for another region. Thus `r` adjacent exact-phase regions give at least `r` distinct diameter endpoints on each side.

No lower bound on KKT multiplier mass is used. No claim is made that the two anchoring diameter edges are disjoint or that their opposite endpoints are distinct.

**Disposition:** proved two-sided diameter anchoring for every exact-phase marker interval, and linear two-sided endpoint growth along exact-phase runs. C-II and Level I remain open.
